  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Time-Invariant Memoryless Systems",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply the time-invariance shift test to memoryless continuous-time system rules by verifying that the fixed pointwise mapping from $x(t)$ to $y(t)$ commutes with input shifts and contains no explicit dependence on t.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Invariant Memoryless Systems",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time-Invariant Memoryless Systems",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A continuous-time system is time invariant when shifting the input by $T$ shifts the output by the same $T$ without changing the rule itself. For a memoryless system, the output at each time depends only on the input at that same time, so the pointwise mapping can be written as $y(t)=f(x(t))$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_T(t)=x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the shifted input $x_T(t)=x(t-T)$ and apply the same pointwise rule to it. The shifted-input output is $y_T(t)=f(x(t-T))$, so the test is whether this matches the original output shifted by $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_T(t)=f(x(t-T))=y(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The comparison path is system then shift: first form $y(t)=f(x(t))$, then replace $t$ with $t-T$ to get $y(t-T)=f(x(t-T))$. When the two paths agree, the memoryless rule is time invariant, and the simplest base case to remember is a constant-gain rule such as $y(t)=a x(t)$, which clearly gives the same shifted output on both paths.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Invariant Memoryless Systems",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Testing a Constant-Gain Memoryless Rule",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Decide whether $y(t)=3x(t)$ is time invariant by comparing the output from $x_T(t)=x(t-T)$ with $y(t-T)$ from the original output.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the shift test on the constant-gain memoryless rule $y(t)=3x(t)$. The shifted input is $x_T(t)=x(t-T)$, so the goal is to compare the shifted-input path with the same rule applied before the shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_T(t)=x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the rule directly to the shifted input. Because the gain is fixed at $3$, the shifted-input path gives $y_T(t)=3x(t-T)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_T(t)=3x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the rule first and shift the output afterward. From $y(t)=3x(t)$, replacing $t$ with $t-T$ gives $y(t-T)=3x(t-T)$, which matches the shifted-input path exactly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=3x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since both paths produce the same expression, the rule is time invariant. This constant-gain case is the clean base case to reuse before testing nonlinear or explicitly time-dependent memoryless rules.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Invariant Memoryless Systems",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Testing a Squaring Memoryless Rule",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Decide whether $y(t)=x^2(t)$ is time invariant by comparing the output from $x_T(t)=x(t-T)$ with $y(t-T)$ from the original output.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the pointwise square rule $y(t)=x^2(t)$, so test it with the same input shift $x_T(t)=x(t-T)$ and compare the shifted-input path to the shifted original output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_T(t)=x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the square rule directly to the shifted input. Because the rule is fixed and pointwise, the shifted-input path gives $y_T(t)=(x(t-T))^2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_T(t)=(x(t-T))^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the rule first and shift the output afterward. From $y(t)=x^2(t)$, replacing $t$ with $t-T$ gives $y(t-T)=(x(t-T))^2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=(x(t-T))^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both paths match exactly, so the square rule is time invariant. The key distinction is that the rule is nonlinear, but it still commutes with the shift because it has no explicit $t$ dependence.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
