        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Spotting Explicit Time Dependence in a Memoryless Rule",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Decide whether $y(t)=t x(t)$ is time invariant by comparing the output from $x_T(t)=x(t-T)$ with $y(t-T)$ from the original output.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the explicit time factor $t$ in the memoryless rule $y(t)=t x(t)$. Use the shift test by comparing the shifted-input path with the output obtained after shifting the original result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x_T(t)=x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the rule to the shifted input first. The shifted-input path is $y_T(t)=t x(t-T)$, because the factor $t$ stays attached to the current time variable.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_T(t)=t x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the rule first and shift the output afterward. From $y(t)=t x(t)$, replacing $t$ with $t-T$ gives $y(t-T)=(t-T)x(t-T)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-T)=(t-T)x(t-T)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two paths do not match, so the rule is not time invariant. The important distinction is that the system is still memoryless, but the explicit $t$ dependence changes under a shift and breaks the commutation test.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
